{"task_id": "Mbpp/84", "solution": "def sequence(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the nth number in the specific Newman Conway sequence as defined in the design.\n    Internally uses a memoized recursive helper class for efficient computation.\n    \"\"\"\n    from typing import Dict\n\n    class NewmanConwaySequence:\n        def __init__(self):\n            # Base cases as specified in the design document\n            self.cache: Dict[int, int] = {0: 1, 1: 1, 2: 1, 3: 1}\n\n        def compute(self, n: int) -> int:\n            if n in self.cache:\n                return self.cache[n]\n            \n            # Recurrence relation for n >= 4 as specified in the design document:\n            # a(n) = a(n - a(n-1)) + a(n - a(n-2)) + a(n - a(n-3))\n            # Note: This specific recurrence might produce negative indices for recursive calls\n            # if a(n-x) grows large, which is not handled within this compute method.\n            val = (\n                self.compute(n - self.compute(n - 1))\n                + self.compute(n - self.compute(n - 2))\n                + self.compute(n - self.compute(n - 3))\n            )\n            self.cache[n] = val\n            return val\n\n    # Input validation as specified in the design document for the public API\n    if not isinstance(n, int) or n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"n must be a non\u2011negative integer\")\n    \n    # Instantiate the engine and compute the result\n    engine = NewmanConwaySequence()\n    return engine.compute(n)"}
