{"task_id": "Mbpp/247", "solution": "def lps(s: str) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the length of the longest palindromic subsequence in the given string.\n    Uses dynamic programming to fill a 2D table.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(s)\n\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # dp[i][j] stores the length of the Longest Palindromic Subsequence (LPS)\n    # for the substring s[i...j].\n    dp = [[0] * n for _ in range(n)]\n\n    # All single characters are palindromes of length 1.\n    for i in range(n):\n        dp[i][i] = 1\n\n    # Fill the DP table for substrings of length 2 to n.\n    # `current_len` represents the length of the substring.\n    for current_len in range(2, n + 1):\n        for i in range(n - current_len + 1):\n            j = i + current_len - 1  # `j` is the end index of the current substring.\n\n            if s[i] == s[j]:\n                # If the characters at the ends match, they contribute 2 to the LPS.\n                # The remaining length comes from the LPS of the inner substring s[i+1...j-1].\n                # For `current_len == 2`, the inner substring is empty, so dp[i+1][j-1] is 0.\n                dp[i][j] = (dp[i + 1][j - 1] if current_len > 2 else 0) + 2\n            else:\n                # If the characters at the ends do not match, the LPS is the maximum of:\n                # 1. LPS of s[i+1...j] (excluding s[i])\n                # 2. LPS of s[i...j-1] (excluding s[j])\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i + 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])\n\n    # The result is the LPS of the entire string s[0...n-1].\n    return dp[0][n - 1]"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "def max_sum(arr):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence in the given array.\n    A bitonic subsequence is first increasing and then decreasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n    if n == 1:\n        return arr[0]\n\n    # lis[i] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at arr[i]\n    lis = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n):\n        lis[i] = arr[i]\n        for j in range(i):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j] and lis[i] < lis[j] + arr[i]:\n                lis[i] = lis[j] + arr[i]\n\n    # lds[i] stores the maximum sum of a decreasing subsequence starting at arr[i]\n    lds = [0] * n\n    for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1):\n        lds[i] = arr[i]\n        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):\n            if arr[i] > arr[j] and lds[i] < lds[j] + arr[i]:\n                lds[i] = lds[j] + arr[i]\n\n    max_bitonic_sum = 0\n    for i in range(n):\n        # A single element can be considered a bitonic subsequence, but we ensure at least two elements for a \"peak\"\n        # However, if there's only one element, lis[i] + lds[i] - arr[i] will be arr[i].\n        # For a general bitonic sequence, the element arr[i] (peak) is included in both LIS and LDS sums.\n        # So we subtract arr[i] once to avoid double counting.\n        current_bitonic_sum = lis[i] + lds[i] - arr[i]\n        if current_bitonic_sum > max_bitonic_sum:\n            max_bitonic_sum = current_bitonic_sum\n\n    return max_bitonic_sum"}
