{"task_id": "Mbpp/283", "solution": "def validate(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the frequency of each digit in the integer is less than or equal to the digit itself.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number < 0:\n        number = abs(number) # Handle negative numbers by taking absolute value\n\n    s_number = str(number)\n    \n    # Manually compute frequencies without importing collections.Counter\n    digit_frequencies = {}\n    for char_digit in s_number:\n        digit = int(char_digit)\n        digit_frequencies[digit] = digit_frequencies.get(digit, 0) + 1\n            \n    for digit, frequency in digit_frequencies.items():\n        if digit == 0:\n            # According to the problem statement \"frequency of each digit in the integer is less than or equal to the digit itself\"\n            # This implies that the digit must be > 0 if it appears, because frequency can never be <= 0.\n            # Thus, if 0 appears, this condition is automatically violated unless we interpret \"digit itself\" as its value.\n            # If the digit is 0, its frequency cannot be <= 0. So, any occurrence of 0 makes the condition false.\n            # The example `validate(1234)` == True implies 0s are not expected to satisfy freq <= 0.\n            # A common interpretation for this kind of problem is that a digit 0 appearing makes it false, as freq(0) >= 1 while 0 itself is 0.\n            # Let's explicitly check and return False if 0 appears as it cannot satisfy freq <= 0.\n            return False\n        if frequency > digit:\n            return False\n            \n    return True"}
