{"task_id": "Mbpp/311", "solution": "def set_left_most_unset_bit(value: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Sets the leftmost unset bit in the binary representation of an integer.\n    The \"leftmost unset bit\" is found by scanning from the highest set bit downwards to 0.\n    \"\"\"\n    # Determine the upper bound for scanning bit positions.\n    # For 0, bit_length is 0. For positive numbers, it's the index of the highest set bit + 1.\n    search_limit = abs(value).bit_length()\n\n    # Iterate from (highest bit index) down to 0.\n    for p in range(search_limit - 1, -1, -1):\n        # If the bit at position 'p' is 0, set it and return the new value.\n        if not (value >> p) & 1:\n            return value | (1 << p)\n\n    # If no unset bit is found in the range [0, search_limit-1],\n    # it means all bits in that range are set.\n    # In this case, set the bit at position 'search_limit'.\n    return value | (1 << search_limit)"}
